<SupportingGuide path="/lawsuits/nec-baby-formula/nicu-feeding-records/" introduction="An NEC case review requires the complete NICU chronology: birth and prematurity records, every feed and fortifier, product identification, symptoms, diagnosis, imaging, surgery, discharge, and later care. This guide organizes records and does not give infant-feeding advice.">
  <section id="complete-nicu-chart">
    <h2>Request the complete NICU chart</h2>
    <p>Ask for the full inpatient chart, not only the discharge summary. Include physician, nursing, dietitian, lactation, pharmacy, respiratory, surgery, infectious-disease, and social-work notes; orders; medication administration records; flowsheets; intake and output; and transfer records.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="feeding-product-identification">
    <h2>Feeding logs and product identification</h2>
    <p>Organize feeding orders and administration entries by date and time. Record maternal milk, donor milk, formula, fortifier, parenteral nutrition, volume, concentration, route, interruptions, and changes. Preserve the manufacturer, full product name, lot or batch number when documented, and the facility source.</p>
    <p>Do not infer a brand from a generic entry such as “formula,” “HMF,” or “fortifier.”</p>
  </section>
  <section id="birth-prematurity">
    <h2>Birth, gestational age, and prematurity records</h2>
    <p>Collect prenatal and maternal history relevant to the chart, delivery records, gestational-age assessment, birth weight, Apgar scores, resuscitation, diagnoses, transfer, and the initial NICU plan. Prematurity and the infant's full medical condition are part of any causation review.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="nec-diagnosis">
    <h2>NEC symptoms, diagnosis, and staging</h2>
    <p>Build a chronology of abdominal findings, feeding tolerance, stool findings, vital signs, laboratory results, cultures, diagnosis, staging, consultations, bowel rest, antibiotics, and clinical changes. Keep a parent's recollection separate from the time-stamped chart.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="imaging-surgery">
    <h2>Imaging, surgery, and pathology</h2>
    <p>Request radiology reports and images, surgical consultation notes, operative reports, pathology, bowel-resection details, ostomy records, complications, and later reversal or reconstruction. The <a href={appeal}>2026 federal appellate opinion</a> illustrates why feeding alternatives and case-specific warning causation can matter in one selected case.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="discharge-later-care">
    <h2>Discharge and later treatment</h2>
    <p>Collect discharge diagnoses, nutrition plans, medications, specialist visits, rehospitalizations, growth and development records, gastrointestinal care, therapies, school or support plans, and documented long-term complications.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="expenses-boundaries">
    <h2>Expenses, family impact, and boundaries</h2>
    <p>Preserve itemized bills, insurance explanations, travel, family leave, wage records, home-care costs, and benefit records. Keep complete originals and do not edit or discard chart material because it seems unfavorable.</p>
    <p>The <a href="/lawsuits/nec-baby-formula/">national NEC baby formula guide</a> tracks the litigation. Product use and an NEC diagnosis do not by themselves establish causation. Feeding and treatment questions belong with qualified clinicians; legal and deadline questions belong with counsel.</p>
  </section>
</SupportingGuide>
